{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a major noble in the Crusader states and played a significant role in the politics of the region. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III was born around 1140 and succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152. \nThe *Early Life* of Raymond III is not well documented but it is known that he was raised in the court of his mother's brother King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. \nRaymond III was a skilled military leader and fought in several battles against the Muslim forces. He also played a key role in the *Battle of Inab* in 1149 where the Crusader forces were defeated by the Zengid army. \nThe *Siege of Tripoli* in 1152 was a significant event in the life of Raymond III where he successfully defended his city against the Zengid army. \nRaymond III also participated in the *Battle of Montgisard* in 1177 where the Crusader forces achieved a significant victory against the Ayyubid army. \nThe *Later Life* of Raymond III is also not well documented but it is known that he died in 1187. \nThe *Succession Crisis* that followed the death of Raymond III was a significant event in the history of the County of Tripoli. \nRaymond III was a major figure in the Crusader states and his military campaigns and battles had a significant impact on the region. \nThe *Relationship with Other Nobles* of the Crusader states was an important aspect of the life of Raymond III. He was related to several other nobles including King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem. \nThe *Legacy* of Raymond III is still debated among historians today. Some historians view him as a skilled military leader who played a key role in the defense of the Crusader states. \nThe *Historical Records* of the life of Raymond III are limited and most of what is known about him comes from the accounts of other historians and nobles of the ti